# The x86 instruction table dialect, read.
#
# `deck/bind/code/linux/operand.tree` is 11,489 lines describing 1,270 x86 instructions, and it is not ordinary
# Term: `force adc` / `start 1, share al` / `write 0x14` are its own vocabulary. The house rule is that a custom
# `.tree` dialect is integrated by defining a MILL, never by teaching the core compiler its heads, so this is that
# mill. See deck/term/CLAUDE.md and note/term/one-parser.md.
#
# THE WHOLE VOCABULARY, counted from the file on 2026-08-30: one top-level head (`force`, 1,270 times) and five
# nested ones (`write` 4,266, `start` 2,384, `shift` 1,760, `state` 373, `slate` 161). Nothing else.
#
#   force <name>        an instruction, by mnemonic
#     start <n>, share <register>    operand n is a fixed register, or a literal (`share 1`) (357)
#     start <n>, field <kind>        operand n is an encoded field (2,027)
#     write <byte>                   a literal opcode byte, hex (2,683)
#     write <text>                   an opcode byte carrying a register, `<0x14+r>` (24)
#     write field, field <kind>      an encoded field in the instruction stream (1,559)
#     shift <flag>, <action>         what it does to a flag: write / cloud / clear / mount, on o s z a p c (1,760)
#     state <n>                      an encoding state (373)
#     slate white                    a marker, one value in the whole table (161)

mine operand
  mine list
    mine any
      mine form, like force
        site force
      mine form, like shelved
        site note

# `note draft`: the marker that keeps the file out of the build until this mill is wired in. Read rather than
# refused, so the grammar describes the file as it actually sits on disk.
mine shelved
  mine term, term note
    mine term
      site mark

# `force <mnemonic>` with its encoding beneath it.
#
# The mnemonic may be a SLASH LIST of synonyms (`shl/sal`, `cmovae/cmovnb/cmovnc`), which the parser already reads
# as one name, so nothing special is needed for it. 151 of the 1,270 carry a `shift white` marker on the head line
# itself, which a comma makes a sibling of the mnemonic.
mine force
  mine term, term force
    mine term
      site name
    mine maybe
      mine form, like force-mark
        site mark
    mine list
      mine any
        mine form, like start-share
          site start
        mine form, like start-field
          site start
        mine form, like write-byte
          site write
        mine form, like write-text
          site write
        mine form, like write-field
          site write
        mine form, like shift-flag
          site shift
        mine form, like state-code
          site state
        mine form, like slate-mark
          site slate

# `start 1, share al`: operand n is a fixed register
mine start-share
  mine term, term start
    mine code
      site place
    mine term, term share
      mine any
        mine term
          site register
        mine code
          site register
    mine maybe
      mine form, like slate-mark
        site slate

# `start 2, field state-8`: operand n is an encoded field
mine start-field
  mine term, term start
    mine code
      site place
    mine term, term field
      mine term
        site kind
    mine maybe
      mine form, like slate-mark
        site slate

# `write 0x14`: a literal opcode byte
mine write-byte
  mine term, term write
    mine code
      site byte

# `write <0x14+r>`: an opcode byte that carries a register in its low bits
mine write-text
  mine term, term write
    mine text
      site byte

# `write field, field mount`: an encoded field in the instruction stream, optionally carrying its own encoding
# state (`write field, field raise` / `state 2`, where the `state` is a SIBLING of the two `field` terms under
# `write`, because a comma pops exactly one level)
mine write-field
  mine term, term write
    mine term, term field
    mine term, term field
      mine term
        site kind
    mine maybe
      mine form, like state-code
        site state

# `shift z, write`: what this instruction does to a flag. Four actions across the table: `write` 1,221, `cloud`
# 298 (leaves it undefined), `clear` 237, `mount` 4. Six flags: o s z a p c.
mine shift-flag
  mine term, term shift
    mine term
      site flag
    mine term
      site action

# `state 16`: an encoding state
mine state-code
  mine term, term state
    mine code
      site code

# `force adc, shift white`: a marker on the instruction itself
mine force-mark
  mine term, term shift
    mine term
      site mark

# `slate white`: a marker
mine slate-mark
  mine term, term slate
    mine term
      site mark
